WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 4 Leader Video
Unit 1, Lesson 4: Logic

1
00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:10.410
Now, you might be asking, why in the world are we talking about logic?

2
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.770
I mean, certainly it makes sense to talk about faith.

3
00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:15.130
Certainly it makes sense to talk about truth.

4
00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:20.750
But why would we take an entire week out to talk about something so strange

5
00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:26.250
in a Bible study about logic? Well, I think it starts

6
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:31.630
with what the writer of Hebrews says in the last part of chapter 5 and the early

7
00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:37.390
part of chapter 6. It says anyone who lives on milk, still an infant,

8
00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:40.190
is not acquainted with the teaching about righteousness.

9
00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:45.850
But solid food is for the mature, who by constant use have trained themselves to

10
00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:51.550
distinguish good from evil. Therefore, let us leave the elementary teachings about

11
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:57.390
Christ and go on to maturity. Peter says much the same thing about leaving

12
00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.350
the idea of spiritual milk and moving on to maturity.

13
00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:05.130
Paul says be transformed in the renewing of your minds and put off the old self and

14
00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:09.250
put on the new self, imitate God, have a mind like Christ.

15
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:15.590
And even Jesus said we need to worship God with

16
00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:21.230
all of our heart, with all of our soul, with all of our mind and with all of our

17
00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:27.150
strength. It's clear throughout Scripture that logic has a place and

18
00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:31.010
that is a tool that shouldn't be considered a threat.

19
00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.750
In fact, it's an incredibly useful tool.

20
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.110
I mean, after all, Jesus said it.

21
00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:40.790
And in fact, you'll find the Bible study this week that Jesus even demonstrated it.

22
00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.730
We'll see a couple of strange things throughout the study.

23
00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:48.750
And one of those things is this weird term that you see in Latin called reductio ad

24
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:49.550
absurdum.

25
00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:54.030
I'll say that again, reductio ad absurdum.

26
00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:59.290
And essentially, it's just a fancy Latin word that means reduce to

27
00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.670
absurdity.

28
00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.550
It's that simple. And even that seems kind of confusing.

29
00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:10.990
So let me give you an example that should help with your students to explain this

30
00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:12.190
kind of strange concept.

31
00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:17.170
When I was a kid, ever so often I would skip school.

32
00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:20.390
And this one time in particular, my dad caught me.

33
00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:23.450
So naturally, he asks me, why did you skip school?

34
00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.870
Well, I had this genius idea, this genius logic that I came up with.

35
00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:33.830
And I told him, well, all my friends were doing it, so that's why I did it.

36
00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:36.050
That makes sense. That made perfect sense in my head.

37
00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.870
And I kind of stood there waiting for him to make a response.

38
00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:43.750
And he didn't take very long before he said, well, if all of your friends jumped off

39
00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:46.810
a bridge, would you too?

40
00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:53.190
Essentially what my dad was doing, whether he realized it or not, was taking my

41
00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:58.910
argument, drawing it out to its natural conclusion, which is absurd.

42
00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:03.550
And that's essentially what reductio ad absurdum is telling us.

43
00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:09.630
And when we see Matthew 22, it's Jesus engaging in this same sort of logical

44
00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:13.170
analysis in his confrontation with the Pharisees.

45
00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.750
So instead of fearing logic,

46
00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:21.890
our goal is to get students to embrace logic and actually get them to understand

47
00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.670
that logic is a vital tool in the search for truth.

48
00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:29.410
Remember what we're doing here throughout this process, and we talked about this

49
00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:33.070
when we talked about truth, is that students are looking for what is true.

50
00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:38.810
We want students at the end of this process to be able to look truth squarely in the

51
00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:43.550
face and actually see Jesus. So logic is going to help them get to that point.

52
00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:49.090
So in their intro experience, we want them first to actually see that in their

53
00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:54.770
relationships. So students are using logic in their relationships to

54
00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:58.750
search for truth in those relationships, and we want them to do the same thing with

55
00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:01.090
Jesus. So take Paul, for example.

56
00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:05.730
If you look through Acts, whether Paul's in Corinth, whether he's in Ephesus,

57
00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:11.250
whether Paul's in Thessalonica or in Athens, what you see is that Paul is constantly

58
00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:16.930
using logic. He's reasoning from the Scriptures about helping them understand

59
00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:20.550
who Jesus is, what he's all about, what salvation means.

60
00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:26.370
Essentially, Paul uses logic through and through in almost every single one of his

61
00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:29.410
arguments as he's traveling on his missionary journeys.

62
00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:35.550
So the idea is we want to invite students into a space where they can be curious,

63
00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:41.050
not only ask the questions, but sort of logically think through these things and add

64
00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:43.010
an intellectual piece to their faith.

65
00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:47.970
So as you dive into small group, you're going to ask what looks like a really simple

66
00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.690
question. Should faith make sense?

67
00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:54.910
That's kind of tricky, right? There's a little bit going on here that we need to

68
00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:59.290
address because there's so much about Jesus that does not seem to make sense.

69
00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:02.290
There's so much about our faith that doesn't seem to make sense sometimes.

70
00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:08.730
But as we think through, as we use logic, as we actually engage

71
00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:13.430
intellectually with our faith, there's a lot we can figure out and God has given us

72
00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:18.270
the tools to do so. And really this is much of the Pharisees' problem.

73
00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:22.750
This is what we see from our question one and their motivation to get after Jesus.

74
00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:24.830
And they just didn't really get it.

75
00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:30.310
But I want to take a second and really touch on question three because this deals

76
00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:34.430
with potential arguments against Christianity and whether or not these arguments

77
00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:35.010
make sense.

78
00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:41.310
Be careful here, but answer these honestly or talk about these honestly because

79
00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:46.210
there are some arguments out there that sound pretty good, that seem to make some

80
00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:49.950
sense. But that doesn't mean that they're logical and doesn't make them right.

81
00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:52.170
It just makes them additional arguments.

82
00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:54.370
But a lot of them are worth talking about.

83
00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:56.910
But make sure, gauge your students.

84
00:05:56.990 --> 00:05:58.630
Make sure you know where they're at.

85
00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:02.230
If you don't think your students can handle it, then that may be a question you

86
00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:05.070
might want to sidestep for just a minute and then come back to it when you think

87
00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:10.010
that they're ready. So as students depart for this week, leave them with this

88
00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:13.790
question. What about your faith doesn't make sense to you?

89
00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:19.950
And challenge them with what it's going to take to spur them on to a mature

90
00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:25.550
faith. Because remember, Jesus calls us all to clear and careful thinking.

91
00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:28.550
The same kind of thinking that Jesus demonstrated.

